{"schema":"plectis_maths_universe_excerpts_v2","paper":"erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface","excerpts":[["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-c14","An equivalent vanishing condition","<p><em> </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mtable displaystyle=\"true\" columnspacing=\"1em\" rowspacing=\"3pt\" data-break-align=\"middle\" minlabelspacing=\"0em\"><mtr><mtd><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mtext> </mtext><mtext>skipped rank </mtext><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn><mtext> whose actual word equals the seam-greedy word</mtext><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mtext>the integer remainder at </mtext><mi>n</mi><mtext> is zero</mtext><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></span></div>\n<p><em> This condition is equivalent to Definition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#record:257bm-c13\">6.81</a>. At an actual\nskipped rank, the source proves that <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span> holds exactly when the real\nprefix agrees with the integer greedy word and its integer remainder is\npositive. On agreement, <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> is\nthe negative of that nonnegative remainder. Thus nonnegativity of the\nmargin at every skip is equivalent to vanishing of the remainder at\nevery aligned skip. The equivalence is proved; neither condition is\nestablished for all required ranks.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-c15","A sufficient lower bound for the\ninteger remainder","<p><em> </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mtext> </mtext><mtext>skipped rank </mtext><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>rem</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> a\nsufficient condition for the nonnegativity condition above. Indeed, a\nnegative margin at an actual skip would force <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>rem</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>,\ncontradicting this bound. This proves the implication, not a converse or\nstrict separation of the conditions.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257rig-c16","A square-root carry bound implies\nhalf-membership","<p><em> Write <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>G</mi></math></span> for\nthe greedy support and <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> for\nits centred carry. If </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> then the greedy selected support <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>G</mi></math></span> is infinite and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>. Nonnegativity of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></math></span> is unconditional (see Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#thm:mobius-centred-nonneg\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:mobius-centred-nonneg\">6.11</a> and the\ngreedy case above); only the upper <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></math></span> bound remains open. This is a different hypothesis from the\ndyadic-band condition and the largest-skip condition; logical\nindependence is not asserted.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257rig-c17","Terminal bounds at unbounded depths","<p><em> Suppose that for every <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>\nthere are <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>M</mi><mo>≥</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">max</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>D</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span> such that </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>ihc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">⌊</mo><msqrt><mi>M</mi></msqrt><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">⌋</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>4.</mn></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Then some infinite <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>A</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></math></span> satisfies\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>. No compatibility\nbetween different finite supports or bound on their earlier carries is\nrequired.</em></p>\n<p><em>These are not the exact-row conditions of Definition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#record:257bm-d4\">6.66</a>. For such a finite\nsupport, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>ihc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em>\nso an exact row has carry <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></span>,\nwhereas the displayed bound allows several positive and negative values.\nAt <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></math></span>, for example, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span> has <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn></math></span> and carry <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn></math></span>: it satisfies the terminal bound but is\nnot an exact row. Both cofinal existence statements nevertheless imply,\nand are implied by, half-membership, as explained in Proposition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#prop:collapsed-list\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#prop:collapsed-list\">2.7</a>. The difference\nbetween their finite data must not be confused with a strict logical\nweakening of the membership problem.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257rig-c18","Cofinal returns of the greedy carry","<p><em> Suppose that for every <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>\nthere is <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>M</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>N</mi></math></span> such that </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>ihc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">⌊</mo><msqrt><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></msqrt><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">⌋</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>4.</mn></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>G</mi></math></span> is infinite and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>. Unlike Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#record:257rig-c16\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257rig-c16\">6.84</a>, this hypothesis\nbounds the actual greedy carry only at unboundedly many indices. It\nstill concerns the same fixed support <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>G</mi></math></span>, not independently chosen finite\nsupports. Comparing this carry with the carry obtained by fixing a\nfinite prefix introduces a contribution from later omitted ranks. No\nequivalence with the finite-support hypothesis of Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#record:257bm-c7\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-c7\">6.75</a> follows without\ncontrolling that additional contribution. The hypothesis is not\nestablished.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-c19","An eventual nonnegative margin\nsuffices","<p><em> Fix a positive depth <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>k</mi></math></span> and put <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>∩</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span>. The following\nconditions are equivalent: </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟺</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext> </mtext><mtext>for some </mtext><mi>J</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.</mn></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Here <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> is the integer expression defined\nin Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#thm:frozen-margin\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:frozen-margin\">6.13</a>.\nIts normalised value is </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em>\nThis normalised expression, not necessarily <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> itself, is nondecreasing in <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>J</mi></math></span>. Its limit is <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, by the finite-support\nLambert identity. This is positive exactly under the strict dyadic\ninequality on the left. At positive <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>k</mi></math></span>, oddness of the reduced excess\nnumerator excludes equality at zero.</em></p>\n<p><em>The result supplies some finite horizon, not the horizon <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></math></span> required in the crossing\napplication. More quantitatively, write <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>. Since <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>, the\nomitted tail shows that <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>η</mi></math></span> suffices for <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>. The value <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>η</mi></math></span> is rational for the finite support\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>D</mi></math></span>, so this is an effective\nsufficient test. Proving it at the prescribed horizon is a separate\narithmetic obligation.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-c20","Half-membership and infinitely many\nskips","<p><em> </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝒜</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟺</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mtext>the set of ranks skipped by the greedy rule for </mtext><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mtext> is infinite</mtext><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Odd-denominator parity\nsupplies the positivity clause in Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#record:257bm-c2\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-c2\">6.68</a> automatically. Thus\nits cofinal-positive-skip hypothesis is exactly the assertion that the\ngreedy skipped support is infinite, which the displayed theorem\nidentifies with half-membership. See Observation <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#record:257bm-k5\">6.132</a> for the reason this\nrestatement does not itself prove membership.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-i1a","The next floor quotient","<p><em> For\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>M</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, the floor quotient satisfies </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em>\nIndeed, <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">⌊</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">⌋</mo></mrow></munderover><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></math></span>. Increasing <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>M</mi></math></span> doubles each existing term and adds\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></span> exactly when <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> is divisible by <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi></math></span>. Thus a quotient doubles or doubles\nplus one; it does not remain unchanged except when both values are zero.\nSumming over a fixed finite support gives the corresponding update with\nadded term <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-i1b","The next quotient sum","<p><em> For a\nfixed finite set <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>D</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>M</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> This is the\nsum of Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#record:257bm-i1a\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-i1a\">6.89</a> over <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi></math></span>. Keeping <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>D</mi></math></span> fixed is essential: changing the\nsupport between steps adds a separate difference of quotient sums. The\nexclusion of <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> is also essential\nfor the displayed correction term, since <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>M</mi></msup></math></span> doubles without an added\nunit.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-i1c","The signed endpoint recurrence","<p><em>\nThe signed next-step expression is <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. The last\nterm counts the selected exponents dividing the new endpoint. This is\nthe same recurrence shape as the generic tempered-orbit recurrence <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> from the\ntempered-orbit criterion (Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#record:257bm-i-t7\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-i-t7\">6.105</a> below),\nspecialised to Mersenne local repair. A matching recurrence alone does\nnot imply rationality: Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#record:257bm-i-t7\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-i-t7\">6.105</a> also requires one\nfixed coefficient sequence, integer states and a vanishing scaled limit.\nThose additional conditions must be checked before that criterion can be\napplied to these finite-row quantities.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-i5","Equivalent forms of the sharper bound","<p><em> Under the hypotheses of Definition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#record:257bm-i-cap\">6.92</a>: </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟺</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>∪</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>c</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> The threshold on the right is one greater than the\nexact-row target <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>. Thus\nthis is a test for omitting rank <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi></math></span>,\nnot for including it in the completed row.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-i6","An unconditional bound with one extra\nbit","<p><em> Unconditionally, for every <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></math></span> and every below-half core <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>D</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> with deficit <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>: </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em>\nThe bound holds uniformly in <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi></math></span>, but\nis twice the threshold needed in Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#record:257bm-i5\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-i5\">6.93</a> to apply\nTheorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#record:257bm-c7\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-c7\">6.75</a>.\nBefore using <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math></span>,\nthe proof gives the sharper additive estimate <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo></math></span>.\nThus the critical-capacity inequality would follow by excluding the\ninteger band <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></math></span>, which contains\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> integers. This is the same band\nshape as Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#record:257bm-c11\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-c11\">6.79</a>’s dyadic-band\ncondition. Its width is linear in <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi></math></span>, whereas the square-root reset\ncondition discussed in Section <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#sec:o4\">12.5</a> has width <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math></span> in a different parameter\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>r</mi></math></span>. Comparing these widths alone\nproves no implication between the two hypotheses.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-i7","The binary bound after doubling","<p><em>\nLet <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>6</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>D</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span> satisfy <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>. Then </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> The missing\nquotient can therefore be filled using only the ranks <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, without changing <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>D</mi></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-i-rank2","Every exact sum contains exponent\ntwo","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></math></span> and let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>D</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span> satisfy <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>. Then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi></math></span>. Thus the rank-two hypothesis in\nTheorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#record:257bm-i7\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-i7\">6.95</a> is\nautomatic for its exact rows. The interval restriction on <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>D</mi></math></span> is part of the assertion, not an\nassumption about arbitrary finite quotient sums.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-i9","Error in the finite subseries\nvalue","<p><em> An exact row at endpoint <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi></math></span> has real value within <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>: the quantitative bound <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>≤</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></math></span> consumed by\nTheorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#record:257bm-c1\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-c1\">6.67</a> to\nturn a sequence of exact rows tending to depth infinity into a sequence\nof values tending to <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-i10","No finite support has value one\nhalf","<p><em> A finite sum of reciprocals of odd integers has odd\ndenominator in lowest terms, so it cannot equal <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>. Applied to the denominators <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, this proves that a support\nrepresenting <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> must be infinite,\nas used in Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#record:257bm-c1\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#record:257bm-c1\">6.67</a>.</em></p>"]],"source":"erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.json?v=09745a86500d"}
